# Interchangeable text providers

`TextProvider.generate(TextRequest) -> TextResult` is shared by fake, OpenAI,
Gemini Developer API and Vertex AI. `GenerateText` depends only on that port.
`app.state.ai` is a lazy runtime adapter; it reads configuration when called.
No public generation HTTP route is created before authentication is implemented.

Only providers selected in `project.yaml` are enabled. The fake implementation
is included for tests, but cannot be selected at runtime unless enabled. There
is no implicit fallback to fake after a provider/configuration error.

## Select a provider without changing domain code

| AI_PROVIDER | Required environment |
| --- | --- |
| fake | None; enabled fake is the default |
| openai | OPENAI_API_KEY, OPENAI_MODEL |
| gemini | GEMINI_API_KEY, GEMINI_MODEL |
| vertex | VERTEX_MODEL, GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT, GOOGLE_CLOUD_LOCATION, Google ADC credentials |

Use an explicit model ID available to your account. No current/latest model is
hardcoded. Simple model IDs (including dots, dashes and colons) are accepted;
custom endpoint URLs and full resource paths are outside this template's scope.
Vertex uses standard project/location plus Application Default Credentials,
not the API-key express mode. Gemini and Vertex use the official Google Gen AI
SDK with explicitly separate client modes. OpenAI uses the official SDK and
Responses API with `store=False` and no automatic tool execution.

Set variables in the shell for direct Python use or in the root `.env` for
Compose. `.env` is ignored by Git and is never copied into an image. Credentials
are supplied only to the API, not the browser. Never put keys into project.yaml.
For local Vertex containers, provide ADC through a deliberate read-only mount
in a local Compose override and set GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS to the
container path. Host credential files are not discovered or mounted by the harness.
Managed workload identity is a later deployment concern.

## Configuration, remote checks and generation are separate

```sh
make ai-check                   # Local validation only; no SDK client/network
make ai-check-remote            # Explicit credential/model-metadata request
printf 'Your input here' | make ai-generate       # Fake by default
printf 'Your input here' | make ai-generate-live  # Explicit real generation
```

Direct Python equivalents, in the API environment:

```sh
python -m app.infrastructure.ai_cli check
python -m app.infrastructure.ai_cli check --remote
python -m app.infrastructure.ai_cli generate --live < input.txt
```

`ai-check` and the readiness probe named `ai_configuration` only verify local
configuration. They **do not validate credentials**, discover ADC or call models.
The remote check retrieves metadata for the configured model and reports a
sanitized result. Metadata access does not prove generation permission, billing,
quota or future availability. A real generation is a separate explicit operation
and can incur charges. The CLI never generates against a real provider without
`--live`. Prompts come from stdin and are not placed in shell command arguments.

Diagnostic output excludes keys, raw SDK errors, prompts, model IDs and project
IDs. Generation output intentionally includes the returned text and configured
model. Errors use stable codes: configuration, authentication, rate_limited,
timeout, unavailable, invalid_request, invalid_response, blocked or incomplete.
SDK diagnostics are disabled by the CLI; do not enable SDK/HTTP debug logging
around private production traffic in your own application.

## Contract and limits

The contract is non-streaming text generation: 1–16000 prompt characters and
16–2048 requested output tokens. These caps do not guarantee cost or success for
every model. Empty text, refusals and truncated/incomplete responses raise a
sanitized error instead of being returned as successful full answers. Google
thought parts are excluded from text. Responses remain model output, not trusted
instructions. No embeddings, tools, agents, structured output or retries are added.

SDK HTTP calls use a 20-second timeout and no automatic retries. Credential
discovery/refresh follows Google Auth behavior and is not an end-to-end deadline.
Clients and transports close after explicit operations. The fake returns a
constant result, not a simulated model or a billing/token estimator.

## Tests and verification scope

`make test` / `make test-contracts` use actual installed SDKs with in-memory HTTP
transports and synthetic credentials. They verify request shape, switching through
the same use case, metadata checks, authentication/rate errors, timeouts,
refusals, truncation, sanitization and no retries without calling providers.
Vertex uses an explicit synthetic OAuth token in these tests; ADC is not accessed.

Live account verification must use the explicit commands above. Passing local
contracts is not evidence of live credentials or model availability. SDK versions
are pinned in requirements and their hashes. Other selected capabilities, such
as SQL/Redis, retain their own contracts and migration commands.

